  "stable_key": "n6-lesson-06",
  "position": 1,
  "title": "Quadratic functions y = ax² + bx + c",
  "summary": "Interpret a quadratic rule, calculate function values accurately, build a value table and connect algebraic features to points on its graph.",
  "outcomes": [
    "6.6"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [],
  "estimated_minutes": 55,
  "objectives": [
    "Identify the coefficients a, b and c in a quadratic function with a non-zero.",
    "Evaluate a quadratic function for positive, zero and negative inputs using correct order of operations.",
    "Construct and check a table of values, then plot the corresponding quadratic graph."
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-06-explanation-1",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Recognise a quadratic function",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A quadratic function assigns exactly one output y to each permitted input x and has highest power x². In the standard form "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "y=ax^2+bx+c"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ", the leading coefficient a must be non-zero; otherwise the x² term disappears and the rule is linear. Coefficients may be positive, negative, fractional or zero, but the exponent on x remains 2."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "y=ax^2+bx+c,\\qquad a\\ne 0"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Read missing terms as zero coefficients. For "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "y=3x^2-5"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ", the coefficients are "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "a=3,\\ b=0\\text{ and }c=-5"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ". For "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "y=x^2-4x+1"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ", the unwritten coefficient of x² is 1. Keeping this standard order makes comparison and substitution easier."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-06-explanation-2",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Evaluate outputs without losing signs",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "To find a function value, replace every x with the stated input and use brackets around a negative input. Square the input before multiplying by a, then calculate the linear and constant terms. The distinction between (-3)²=9 and -3²=-9 is a common source of errors."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "f(-3)=a(-3)^2+b(-3)+c=9a-3b+c"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A table of values is a collection of ordered pairs (x,y). Inputs chosen on both sides of zero often reveal the curve clearly. Recalculate any output that breaks an expected symmetric pattern before plotting it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-06-explanation-3",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Connect the rule, table and graph",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Each column in a value table gives one point on the graph. For y = x² − 4, the table below lists seven inputs from −3 to 3 and their outputs."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "n6-lesson-06-table"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Plot x horizontally and y vertically, use a consistent scale, and draw a smooth curve through the calculated points. A quadratic graph is a parabola rather than a chain of straight line segments."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "n6-lesson-06-graph"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For y = x² − 4, the point (0, −4) is the y-intercept because x = 0. The points (−2, 0) and (2, 0) are x-intercepts because y = 0. Substituting each coordinate into the original rule verifies that it lies on the graph. The lowest point, (0, −4), is the turning point, also called the vertex."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-06-example-1",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Build a table of values",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "y=2x^2-3x-2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ", find y when "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "x=-1,\\ 0,\\ 1\\text{ and }2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Substitute "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "latex": "x=-1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " with brackets."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "y=2(-1)^2-3(-1)-2=2+3-2=3"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Evaluate the remaining inputs in the same order."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\begin{array}{c|rrrr}x&-1&0&1&2\\\\\\hline y&3&-2&-3&0\\end{array}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The four graph points are (-1,3), (0,-2), (1,-3) and (2,0)."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "Substituting x=2 into the original rule gives 8-6-2=0, matching the last table entry."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-06-example-2",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Find a missing coefficient from a point",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The graph of "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "y=x^2+bx+6"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " passes through "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "(2,4)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ". Find b."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Because "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "latex": "(2,4)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " lies on the graph, substitute "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "latex": "x=2"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "latex": "y=4"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "4=2^2+2b+6"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Simplify and solve the resulting linear equation."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "4=10+2b\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 2b=-6\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad b=-3"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The function is therefore "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "latex": "y=x^2-3x+6"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "Check the point: when x=2, y=4-6+6=4, so (2,4) satisfies the completed rule."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-06-recall",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "f(x)=-2x^2+5x+1"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ", find "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "f(-2)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "-17",
        "canonical_latex": "-17",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use brackets: "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "f(-2)=-2(-2)^2+5(-2)+1=-8-10+1=-17"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-06-summary",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Lesson checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Confirm that the highest power is 2 and identify a, b and c in standard order, remembering that a cannot be zero."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Bracket negative inputs, calculate powers before multiplication, plot each table row as an ordered pair, and verify suspicious points in the original rule."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
